  steps: [
    { math: '1 · All humans are mortal', note: 'Premise one. The "All …" shape is what gives the argument its power — it covers every human, including the one in premise two.' },
    { math: '2 · Socrates is human', note: 'Premise two places Socrates inside the first premise\'s net. Notice nothing here has been asserted about mortality yet.' },
    { math: '∴ Socrates is mortal', note: 'The conclusion is forced: if you accept 1 and 2, there is nowhere else to stand. This shape is a syllogism — Aristotle\'s catalogue, ~350 BCE.' },
    { math: 'If P → Q, and P, then Q', note: 'Modus ponens, the workhorse of logic — and of every computer program: condition true, consequence follows.' },
