<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/logic-arguments">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An argument, in philosophy, is not a quarrel — it is a structure: <strong>premises</strong>
    offered in support of a <strong>conclusion</strong>. Logic is the study of which structures
    actually hold. Its two verdicts are different and both matter: an argument is
    <strong>valid</strong> if the conclusion follows from the premises <em>if they were true</em>;
    it is <strong>sound</strong> if it is valid <em>and</em> the premises really are true.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the classic syllogism, assembled step by step" widget="equationStep" config={sylCfg} />

  <h2>The two ways arguments fail</h2>
  <p>
    Either a premise is false, or the shape leaks. Shaped failures have names — knowing them is
    armour in any debate:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Affirming the consequent:</strong> "If rain, the ground is wet. The ground is wet. Therefore it rained." Invalid — a sprinkler explains it too.</li>
    <li><strong>Denying the antecedent:</strong> "If rain, wet ground. No rain. Therefore dry ground." Also invalid — same sprinkler.</li>
    <li><strong>Ad hominem:</strong> attacking the arguer instead of the argument. A liar can still state true things.</li>
    <li><strong>Straw man:</strong> refuting a claim nobody made. <strong>False dilemma:</strong> offering two options where more exist. <strong>Appeal to authority:</strong> expertise lends weight, not proof.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why this is practical, not pedantic</h2>
  <p>
    George Boole reduced logic to algebra in 1854 (<em>The Laws of Thought</em>), and in 1937
    Claude Shannon showed the same algebra describes electrical switches — and the digital
    computer fell out of a philosophy problem. Every <code>if</code> statement in every program
    is modus ponens wearing a compiler.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Aristotle, Prior Analytics (c. 350 BCE); Boole (1854); Shannon, "A Symbolic Analysis of Relay and Switching Circuits" (1937).</p>
</TopicPage>
